{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The countercondition 'If the test's purpose is specifically to detect duplication or over-commitment, monotonicity alone would not catch an unwanted increase' concedes that monotonicity fails to catch a whole class of regressions the original exact-count test was plausibly there to catch. The problem asks what general property such a test 'should assert instead'; an invariant that, by the target's own admission, cannot detect duplication or over-commitment is not a faithful general replacement for an exact-count regression test. This is a substantive content defect, not a machine-checked commitment violation.","target":"41c67c4cd8e77b5771c7b600ab9cb529b118dce99722a9ec5c9b13776123cf5e"}]}